  {
    "episode_id": 70874,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/0/70874.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "Well, we did a lot of changes really dating back to at the end of my freshman term. I decided to challenge the long serving minority whip to be the whip for the conference coming into my second term. And a lot of that was premised on the need to democratize our conference. Over time, we'd better the minority for all but one other instance in 100 years. We had this mentality where only a few people really controlled the agenda for the conference. So we spent a lot of time democratizing the conference to get more people engaged, to get more out of the member, more leverage out of the individual members. Probably seeing that happen when we were in the minority is probably what laid the groundwork for the members to feel comfortable with elected me to speaker because we did the same thing. We completely revamped the way the house operations worked and up to an including revamping the rules to show better respect to the minority. It's one of the reasons why I think we got such good cooperation on some key bills when we had to then Democrat governor who was vetoing some of our key reform measures, and we overrode them with Democrat",
