    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/0/70874.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 31,
    "turn_text": "Except for the 18 out of 52 members who voted with us on one or more of 11 veto overrides, they didn't do that for fun. They did it because the policy that we were negotiating, they were all transactional sort of bipartisanship, so it wasn't all on the same. measures, but I think that that was an indication of we were willing to go where we needed to to get bipartisan support for some pretty heavy left reforms that are showing the impact today. And, of course, there are going to be instances where I'm a conservative, they're liberal, we're not going to agree, we stay away from those things, but I've collaborated with some of the most liberal members on education reform, for example. and with fiscal conservatives on tax reform and on budget reform. It was more a matter of just trying to figure out how you create that unique set of policy that would cast a wide enough net to move forward when I didn't have a super majority.",
