    "turn_text": "part of it, it's just like up here in DC. When people are fundamentally opposed to the policy, they start screaming process. But the fact of the matter is, we didn't bring a single rule. We loved by the rules. In fact, when we revised the rules, we made it difficult to move things through very quickly. We created a rule where, if a conference report the old day was, the Democrat leadership would appear to be kind of going through the process, then they'd create a conference report. They would stuff everything in the conference report, bring it back to the floor with no notice whatsoever and force you to vote. And for some reason, that didn't seem like a bad policy. I thought it was, which is why we changed the rules to require any conference report that had substantially different language than when either of the two bills, it had to go back to a committee. before it could be voted on the floor. So I'd be people cop out to the process narrative. Quite honestly, I don't think most people in the United States care about",
