    "turn_idx": 72,
    "turn_text": "North Carolina. Had some sort of up or down vote to come up with a new process. When I became speaker, I was also for it. Unfortunately, I couldn't get my caucus or the Senate. to agree but me and the then minority or majority leader were vocally supportive of a different way of doing redistricting to get it out of the courts and and I get it into a process that we think that most people in North Carolina want it so I've got a pretty good track record of not changing up to an including when I became speaker changing the the Senate rule or the House rules. We got a unanimous vote on the first permanent House rules that we did when I became Speaker. Again, the second outright elected Republican Speaker in 100 years. It was a huge number of reasons to think the Democrats were even going to make the permanent rules difficult. The reason they voted for them is that what was fundamental to our rules were formed were repealing all the rules the majority had passed without any support from minority that made my job easier. And I told my colleagues I'm perfectly content to learn how to use the rules and do it in a way that doesn't let me hide behind all of these artificial disingenuous tools that a speaker had where he didn't have to work hard. And so I think that's why we got such a strong vote in the rules and And I think that's one of the reasons why a great relationship with the vast majority of Democrats, some of whom I would never be voting with on the same side, but at least we showed them some respect in the",
