    "turn_idx": 114,
    "turn_text": "unless you're for nationalizing the banking system unless you hate all big businesses because they're bad and evil, you're not a true Democrat. So they've got the same. In some respects, I think they have a little bit more complexity because there's two or three groups that are coalescing around a set of ideas that if you're not just like them, you're not a true Democrat, you saw that play out with Bernie. and with Clinton and the campaign. So I think in some respects, we've got the group of us who are sound conservatives have a track record on it, but recognize that perfect never gets done. A good outcome is better than no outcome and perfect outcomes don't happen here. So I think in some respects, we have a smaller group of people, we have to get on board on a transactional basis. You know, I'm sure that over the course of my tenure, I may have a vote that I feel so strongly about at a conscience level that I may disagree with the leadership and vote a different way. But that's not my go-to position. I told all my members when I was a minority weapon speaker, I said, you got three Cs to come up with how you're going to vote on a bill. You always vote your conscience, even if it's in conflict with your constituents. Never take that step down the slippery slope. If your conscience isn't driving you and most of this stuff isn't about conscience, listen to your constituents. And only if your conscience or your constituents are not telling you how to vote, then listen to your conference. I called it the three C's and said, you know, look, if you're at ads with the conference, just tell the leader you're at odds with them and vote.",
