    "turn_text": "I think that the stabilizing the markets and trying to get to that trend of more state-focused initiatives is really what the White House wants to see. We're going to have some difference of opinion about the cost-sharing reductions, but the reality is The industry, the insurers, the providers, everybody else has had seven years to deal with Obamacare. And this policy, whether you agree with it or not, is a part of the underlying foundation of health care for the population affected by the Affordable Care Act. So you just can't overnight take that away. You have to have some way to give a year or two of transition time so that the market can react appropriately and factor in all the other changes that would occur after this first bills pass. So the stuff that Senator Alexander is working on with Senator Murray is something I'm open to. I haven't committed to support it. Certainly, I haven't said that I oppose it,",
